class Solution {
  public int[] positiveTraysPositiveProofStrip(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {for(int x:a)if(x>0)out.add(x);}
    return out.stream().filter(x->x>0).mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build kept

Keep only numbers greater than 0 from `readings`. Put them in `kept` in their original order. Keep repeated numbers. Leave out 0 and negative numbers.

Return the answer

Create a list `positiveValues` that keeps only the numbers in `kept` that are greater than 0, in the same order. Complete `positiveTraysPositiveProofStrip` so it returns `positiveValues`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `kept` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

